  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH FRIDAY/...\nIssued at 309 AM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nOur local weather will be driven primarily by two features through \nthe extended period: upper-level ridging encompassing the eastern \n2/3rds of the CONUS, and a surface high centered over the Southeast. \nThese features will combine to keep flow over the Middle Mississippi \nValley southwesterly through the depth of the atmosphere, which will \nprovide a continuous feed of abnormally warm air. The heart of the \nthermal ridge builds aloft Sunday, characterized by 850 mb \ntemperatures in the low 20s degrees C, and is fully realized Monday \nthrough Friday. During this stretch, a majority of the CWA will \nexperience afternoon air temperatures peaking in the mid to upper \n90s, with up to a 30% chance per the NBM of highs reaching 100 \ndegrees. \n\nA mitigating factor for dangerous heat levels will be dew points. \nGiven some degree of southwesterly surface flow through this \nstretch, we will have to contend with downsloping from the Ozarks, \nwhich will serve to reduce dewpoints over southeastern Missouri and \ninto the St. Louis metro area. Additionally, we have passed peak \nevapotranspiration season locally, and with a seasonable lack of \nwidespread recent rainfall, vegetation and soil moisture will do \nlittle to modify dew points. All of this to say, currently \nforecasted dew points may be too high, and if that is the case, some \nlocations will see air temperatures a couple degrees warmer than \nforecast. This shift in balance between air temperatures and dew \npoints will simply lead to similar heat index values to what is \ncurrently forecast. As such, scattered instances of around 100 \ndegrees can be expected. If locations were to need a heat headline, \nit would be for duration (heat index of 100+ degrees for four or \nmore days). Given the current forecast and considering local \nclimatology, confidence is low at this time that a Heat Advisory \nwill be needed.",
